WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.879
A practical hardening roadmap starts with diagnostics.

2
00:00:02.879 --> 00:00:11.104
Raise warning quality, decide which warnings are treated as defects, control suppressions, and make the diagnostic policy consistent across local

3
00:00:11.104 --> 00:00:12.338
builds and CI.

4
00:00:12.338 --> 00:00:15.628
Clean builds make real problems easier to see.

5
00:00:15.628 --> 00:00:19.329
Next, add sanitizer coverage where it fits the project.

6
00:00:19.329 --> 00:00:25.498
Address, undefined behavior, leak, and thread checks help expose defects that ordinary tests may miss.

7
00:00:25.498 --> 00:00:31.255
The value comes from meaningful tests, realistic workloads, prompt triage, and repeatable CI jobs.

8
00:00:31.255 --> 00:00:38.658
Then define release hardening settings for stack protection, object-size checks, executable layout, memory permissions, relocation behavior, and

9
00:00:38.658 --> 00:00:41.948
control-flow protection where the platform supports them.

10
00:00:41.948 --> 00:00:48.116
Treat toolchain-specific flags as mappings from security intent, not as copy-and-paste guarantees.

11
00:00:48.116 --> 00:00:51.406
Use runtime and standard library hardening where appropriate.

12
00:00:51.406 --> 00:00:53.874
Development profiles can favor strict checking.

13
00:00:53.874 --> 00:00:57.164
Release profiles should balance risk, compatibility, and performance.

14
00:00:57.164 --> 00:01:02.510
Every profile should be documented so teams know what evidence each one provides.

15
00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:10.324
Finally, verify outputs in CI, inspect delivered artifacts, document exceptions, and revisit the baseline as compilers, platforms, dependencies, and

16
00:01:10.324 --> 00:01:11.557
product requirements evolve.

17
00:01:11.557 --> 00:01:14.848
Compiler security features are part of disciplined engineering.

18
00:01:14.848 --> 00:01:18.960
They support safer C++ design; they do not replace it.
